Under New York law, your landlord had **14 days after you moved out** to return your deposit. If you moved out on August 31, that deadline was around **September 14**. If the landlord is keeping any of it, they also had to send you an itemized list of deductions within those same 14 days.

**What happens if they missed it:**
- **They may lose the right to keep any of it.** If the landlord didn't send the itemized list on time, or didn't send one at all, they generally can't hold back any part of the deposit.
- **You could get up to twice the deposit.** If a court finds the landlord withheld it on purpose, it can award extra damages of up to twice the deposit amount.

**Other rules that may matter:**
- **Deposit limit:** Since 2019, deposits and advance payments together can't be more than one month's rent.
- **Inspections:** The landlord must offer a joint inspection at move-in, with a signed agreement on any existing damage. Before you move out, you have the right to an inspection with notice, a list of proposed deductions and a chance to fix problems.

**Possible next steps:**
1. Send the landlord a written demand letter, ideally by certified mail. Cite N.Y. General Obligations Law § 7-108 and the 14-day deadline, and give them a firm date to pay.
2. If they still don't pay, consider small claims court. In NYC, the limit is $10,000 ([NY Courts small claims info](https://www.nycourts.gov/node/1051)).
3. The [NY Attorney General's tenants' rights guide](https://ag.ny.gov/publications/residential-tenants-rights-guide) is another useful resource.

